(function(){/* ===================================================================
   USER MANUAL — patient chapters.

   Every topic is illustrated with a REAL screenshot of the live app
   (captured from the product itself, stored in assets/manual/) —
   never a mock. Each function is explained three ways: what it IS,
   the real-life PROBLEM it solves, and exactly HOW to use it.

   Screenshot set refreshed 10 Aug 2026 (IMG_69xx captures) and
   completed 11 Aug 2026: img-settings.png (the real Settings screen),
   img-entertainment.png and img-healthsync.png. Only the daily-reminder
   capture is still pending — its topic picks it up by filename.

   All copy goes through tr() so the existing i18n pipeline can
   translate it; English is the source. New strings introduced in this
   revision are translated in i18nmanual2026a/b.jsx.
   =================================================================== */function cfManualPatient(){return[/* ---------- 1. GETTING STARTED ---------- */{id:'start',icon:'leaf',grad:['#7bd853','#3f8a3f'],title:tr('Getting started'),intro:tr('Creating your account takes about a minute. Here is exactly what happens the first time you open Chronic Friends — and how to make it yours.'),topics:[{title:tr('Create your account'),shot:'img-consent.png',body:tr('Your account is your own private space. It safely keeps your journal, medications, Flare Radar and friends, and lets you sign in from any device without losing anything.'),problem:tr('Life with a chronic illness scatters your health story across memory, sticky notes and half-remembered appointments. One secure account gathers it in a single place that is always with you.'),steps:[tr('Open the app and choose <strong>I\u2019m a patient</strong>.'),tr('At the top of the white panel — just above <strong>Welcome back</strong> — tap <strong>Create your account</strong>, then enter your email and a password of at least <strong>6 characters</strong>.'),tr('Type the <strong>6-digit code</strong> we email you to confirm it\u2019s really you.'),tr('Right after the welcome, one screen explains in plain words what is stored (only what you write down), who can see it (only you) and that you can delete all of it from Settings whenever you want. Tick <strong>Yes, store my health data so the app can work</strong> and tap <strong>Accept and continue</strong>.'),tr('The setup assistant (onboarding) starts automatically — name, health details, a quick tour, and your first check-in.')],tip:tr('Signing in never creates an account by itself. If we don\u2019t recognise your email, we ask you to create an account first — so you never end up in an empty duplicate.')},/* VERIF (1 Sep 2026) — the pioneer gift is handed out by the server and
         the server only talks to a verified account, so somebody who never
         verified could lose 14 months without ever being told. The notice on
         Home says it in time; the manual explains why it is there. The button
         name inside <strong> comes from the live dictionary
         (i18nfounderverify). NO shot: — no capture of the notice exists yet. */{title:tr('Verifying your email (and your pioneer months)'),where:[tr('Home')],body:tr('When you create your account we e-mail you a link. Following it is what proves the account is yours — and the pioneer gift, <strong>14 free months</strong> for the first 500 accounts, can only reach a verified account. Until you verify, Home carries a notice with a <strong>Send the email again</strong> button. It grows firmer as the 30 trial days run out, and it disappears by itself the moment you verify.'),problem:tr('The number that makes somebody a pioneer is given out by our server, and the server only talks to a verified account. Without a warning, somebody who never verified would reach day 30 and drop to the free plan having been a pioneer all along.'),tip:tr('Nothing here is granted by your phone’s clock — a date on a device is not proof of anything. If the e-mail never arrives, look in your spam folder and tap <strong>Send the email again</strong>.')},{title:tr('Your details & your plan'),shot:'img-settings.png',where:[tr('Settings')],body:tr('Tap your avatar on Home to open Settings. Your <strong>health details</strong> (weight, height, age, years with your condition) and your <strong>membership</strong> live here. Every screen works the same on any plan — what Premium changes is depth: your whole history instead of your last 14 days, the long reports, and no counters. Any recurring plan starts with a <strong>30-day free trial</strong>.'),problem:tr('Generic health tools ignore who you actually are. Your real details let every chart, report and Flare Radar reading be about <em>your</em> body — not an average patient.'),steps:[tr('Tap <strong>Edit</strong> on \u201cYour details\u201d to keep weight, height, age and years with your condition up to date.'),tr('Set your <strong>country</strong> — when doctors and video visits arrive, it will decide which licensed doctors you can see and book.'),tr('Check <strong>Membership</strong> to see your trial, or to manage or cancel your plan anytime.')],tip:tr('Part of every subscription is donated to research centres and hospitals fighting chronic diseases.')},{title:tr('Finding your condition in the list'),where:[tr('My Health')],body:tr('The setup assistant asks which conditions you live with, and the list uses each one\u2019s medical name. A diagnosis usually has several names, so the search knows those too: type what your report says \u2014 <strong>iritis</strong>, <strong>Bechterew</strong>, <strong>irritable bowel</strong>, <strong>HLA-B27</strong> \u2014 and the row still appears, in your own language. And <strong>\u201cMy condition isn\u2019t listed\u201d</strong> is always there at the bottom, even when the search finds nothing at all.'),problem:tr('A list of medical names is a wall for anyone whose diagnosis is written differently \u2014 and it arrives in the first minute, before the app has shown what it can do. Searching by the name you actually use gets you past it.'),tip:tr('Choosing conditions is never a diagnosis: it only decides which trackers your Journal shows, and you can change them any time in <strong>Settings \u203a My Health</strong>.')},/* [NOCOND] (2 Sep 2026) — the conditions screen now has a row for
         somebody who lives with none, so Continue no longer waits for an
         illness to be ticked. Every <strong> label was taken FROM the live
         dictionary (i18nmodules: the new row + «Prefer to skip…» +
         Digestion · i18n6/i18n8-17: Continue · i18nmanual2026o:
         «Settings › My Health»). NO shot: — no capture exists yet. */{title:tr('If you don\u2019t live with a chronic condition'),body:tr('At the bottom of the list, where the search can never hide it, there is a row of its own: <strong>I don\u2019t live with any chronic condition</strong>. Tick it and <strong>Continue</strong> lights up like with any other answer. No illness is stored, and your journal keeps every everyday question \u2014 sleep, water, steps, sun, time in nature, singing, dancing, mood; only the <strong>Digestion</strong> questions stay out of it. You can add a condition whenever you want in <strong>Settings \u203a My Health</strong>.'),problem:tr('Until now the screen only moved on once an illness had been ticked. Someone caring for a partner or a relative \u2014 or anyone who simply wants to look after their own days \u2014 had to claim a diagnosis they do not have: a false line in their own diary, written in the first minute of the app.'),tip:tr('It is not the same as <strong>Prefer to skip \u2014 I\u2019ll choose trackers myself</strong>, which is still there. Living with no condition and living with one you would rather not write down yet are two different things, and each has its own way through.')}]},/* ---------- 2. HOME ---------- */{id:'home',icon:'home',grad:['#62c96a','#2e7d36'],title:tr('The Home screen'),intro:tr('Home is your calm space — a serene overview of your day. Everything that matters is one tap away.'),topics:[{title:tr('Your Home at a glance'),shot:'IMG_6943.png',where:[tr('Home')],body:tr('Home opens on a living forest scene that grows as you log good days. From top to bottom: your <strong>avatar</strong> and name (tap it for Settings), the <strong>Flare Mode</strong> switch, your live <strong>Flare Radar</strong>, your <strong>Steps today</strong>, the <strong>Check-In</strong> button, <strong>Food Scan</strong> and your <strong>food journal</strong>, the <strong>Meditation</strong>, <strong>Entertainment</strong> and <strong>Tools</strong> cards, your <strong>upcoming appointments</strong> and the <strong>Health Sync</strong> invitation. Your friends live in the <strong>Community</strong> tab.'),problem:tr('Managing a chronic condition means juggling symptoms, food, medication, mood and appointments at once. Home pulls them into one gentle view so nothing important slips through the cracks.')},{title:tr('The Flare Mode switch'),shot:'IMG_6944.png',where:[tr('Home')],body:tr('Right on the forest sits the <strong>Flare Mode</strong> switch. Having a flare-up? Turn it on and the app becomes a calmer, quieter shelter focused on rest, hydration and gentle tracking. Turn it off whenever you feel the flare is over — those days stay marked in <strong>cyan</strong> on your calendar and charts.'),problem:tr('On the hardest days a busy app is the last thing you need. One switch strips everything back to what helps — and because it\u2019s a switch, ending the flare is as deliberate as starting it.'),tip:tr('Only you can turn Flare mode on or off — the app never decides for you.')},{title:tr('The emergency medical card'),where:[tr('Home'),tr('Flare Mode'),tr('Medical card')],body:tr('Fill it in once and it is one tap away for as long as the flare lasts: your condition, the medication you take right now (immunosuppressants included), allergies, blood type, emergency contact, insurance and your doctor — with a photo of your card if you want one. It is kept on this phone and sent nowhere.'),problem:tr('In an emergency nobody types, and nobody should have to look up a number. A responder needs the facts in seconds \u2014 and you need to be able to CALL: the contact and doctor numbers are real dial buttons, so one tap opens your phone\u2019s own dialer with the number already in it.'),tip:tr('You wrote this card yourself: it isn\u2019t verified and it isn\u2019t medical advice. And Chronic Friends can never call emergency services for you \u2014 for that, use your own phone.')}]},/* ---------- 3. JOURNAL & CHECK-IN ---------- */{id:'journal',icon:'cal',grad:['#7bd853','#3f8a3f'],title:tr('Journal & daily check-in'),intro:tr('The Journal is the heart of Chronic Friends. Log your day in seconds and watch real patterns appear over the weeks.'),topics:[{title:tr('Your journal, day by day'),shot:'IMG_6947.png',where:[tr('Journal')],body:tr('The Journal tab holds a calendar where every logged day gets a colour — green for good days, amber for mixed, red for hard ones, and <strong>cyan for Flare mode days</strong> (the legend sits right under the month). Above the calendar sit two buttons: <strong>PDF</strong> and <strong>Analytics</strong>. Tap any day to see or edit exactly what you logged.'),problem:tr('\u201cHow have you been since last time?\u201d is almost impossible to answer honestly from memory. A coloured calendar turns months of ups and downs into something you can actually see and talk about.'),steps:[tr('Open the <strong>Journal</strong> tab in the bottom bar.'),tr('Tap a day to open its entry; swipe between months to look back.'),tr('Coloured days are already saved — grey days are still empty.')]},{title:tr('The daily check-in'),shot:'IMG_6948.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Check-In')],body:tr('Tap the big <strong>Check-In</strong> button once a day. It opens today\u2019s entry and saves automatically as you go, and the questions <strong>adapt to your conditions</strong> — tracker questions appear only for the trackers you keep on. In the evening the app asks you about the same day one question at a time, so this form is here for when you feel like writing, not the only way in.'),problem:tr('The details that explain a flare — a bad night\u2019s sleep, a trigger food, extra stress — are forgotten within days. A 30-second daily habit turns those fleeting details into a record you and your doctor can trust.'),tip:tr('Everything is optional. Log as much or as little as you like — even a single number is worth saving.')},/* 7 Sep 2026 — the second way of filling the journal. The manual quotes
         the app: the three button names inside <strong> (Skip · Next · OK)
         come from the live dictionary (i18n-flare8 · i18nlandscape ·
         i18nckflow), never translated a second time by hand. Nothing else in
         these two topics quotes a label — a label we cannot verify in all 16
         languages is a label the manual must not type.
         NO shot: — no capture of the flow exists yet. */{title:tr('One question at a time'),where:[tr('Journal'),tr('Check-In')],body:tr('In the evening your reminder opens the quick way to log a day: <strong>one question per screen, one tap</strong>. The question fills the screen, the answers are big enough for a thumb, and your tap goes straight to the next one — no scrolling, no keyboard, and a counter at the top so you can see the end from the beginning. The bar shows how far you are, the arrow goes back, the cross leaves — and whatever you answered is already saved.'),problem:tr('The comfortable way to log a day used to be a blue button that copied yesterday: two taps for twenty-three answers nobody had read, feeding your charts and the report your doctor reads with a day that had not been lived. Now the comfortable way is also the true one — 30 to 60 seconds, every answer yours.'),steps:[tr('Tap the notification in the evening, or the bell beside your check-in button, and the first question appears.'),tr('Tap your answer — the next question comes by itself.'),tr('Nothing to say about a question? <strong>Skip</strong>, at the bottom, passes it by without recording anything — the skin questions on a day your skin is fine, for example.'),tr('Where you can tick several answers, tap them all and then <strong>Next</strong>.'),tr('At the end, a quiet line offers back anything you skipped — only if you left something.')],tip:tr('Steps, sleep and activity are not asked when your phone already knows them: the screen shows you the real number and <strong>OK</strong> moves on — and a line underneath lets you say it yourself if you disagree. Writing (the pain comment and the tracker notes) stays in the journal form, where a keyboard belongs.')},{title:tr('A flare you log yourself'),where:[tr('Journal')],body:tr('The first question of the evening asks whether you are having a flare-up, and a yes then asks which areas. That day turns <strong>cyan</strong> on your calendar, appears in the month legend, counts in your reports and comes out cyan in the PDF your doctor reads — exactly like a day spent in Flare Mode.'),problem:tr('Until now a flare day was only recorded by turning Flare Mode on, so anybody who does not use the shelter never recorded a single flare — and those are the days a doctor most needs to see. A flare you answer by hand is now worth exactly as much as one spent in the shelter. It only ever adds: answering no can never take the cyan off a day you really did spend in Flare Mode.')},{title:tr('Rate pain, energy & more'),shot:'IMG_6948.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Check-In')],body:tr('Move the <strong>0\u201310 sliders</strong> for pain and energy, then add whatever applies: sleep, meals, hydration, stress, bowel movements, flare symptoms and substances (you can pick more than one). Finish by choosing your mindset — or your <strong>inner weather</strong>, if your Mind &amp; Mood tracker is on.'),problem:tr('\u201cA bit better\u201d means nothing to a specialist. Consistent 0\u201310 numbers turn your experience into data your doctor can compare week to week — and that quietly feeds your charts, your reports and your Flare Radar.'),steps:[tr('Answer the first question about your day — its wording follows the condition you follow, and the answer is <strong>Good day</strong> or <strong>Tough day</strong>. With an alcohol or substance condition it also asks for <strong>Clean</strong> or <strong>Slip</strong>.'),tr('Rate <strong>pain</strong> and <strong>energy</strong> from 0 to 10.'),tr('Tap the chips for sleep, food, hydration, stress and symptoms.'),tr('Log any <strong>flare symptoms</strong> and <strong>substances</strong> — more than one is fine.'),tr('Finish with your <strong>mindset</strong> — or your <strong>inner weather</strong> when the Mind &amp; Mood tracker is on. Either way, the entry is already saved.')]},{title:tr('Charts & a doctor-ready PDF'),where:[tr('Journal'),tr('Analytics')],body:tr('Tap <strong>Analytics</strong> and every parameter you log — pain, energy, sleep, weight and more — becomes a chart over time. Tap <strong>PDF</strong> to turn it all into a clean medical summary you can hand to your doctor or share live during a video visit.'),problem:tr('Appointments are short and memory is unreliable, so real trends get lost. Charts reveal the patterns behind your symptoms, and the PDF says in one minute what would take twenty to explain.'),tip:tr('Reports of your last 3, 7 or 14 days are free for everyone; Premium reports reach back up to two years.')},{title:tr('Every chip you tap is counted'),where:[tr('Journal'),tr('Analytics')],body:tr('The questions where you can tap several answers at once — <strong>Anything else today?</strong> in Eyes, <strong>Where do you feel it?</strong> in Joints, and the ones for skin, bathroom, breathing and mind — now come back to you as plain counts. In <strong>Analytics</strong>, and in the <strong>PDF</strong> your doctor reads, each tracker’s own section lists what you tapped and on how many days: <strong>Eye pain · 12</strong>, <strong>Redness · 9</strong>, <strong>Blurry vision · 4</strong> — with the most recent days that carry a note written underneath.'),problem:tr('Until now those answers were saved and then shown nowhere: you were recording eye pain for your ophthalmologist and it reached no chart, no report and no PDF. Counting is the honest way to show them — a chip has no 0–10 order, so it is counted, never scored.'),steps:[tr('Tap the chips as you always do in your daily check-in.'),tr('Open <strong>Analytics</strong>, or the <strong>PDF</strong>, and look inside that tracker’s section.'),tr('A chip you have never tapped simply does not appear.')],tip:tr('Counting is all that happens here: no severity, no colour, no arrow, no week-against-week comparison. The number is yours to explain.')},{title:tr('Eye episodes — which eye, and how it ended'),where:[tr('Journal'),tr('Eyes')],body:tr('In the Eyes tracker, <strong>An episode is starting</strong> now asks one optional question first: <strong>Right eye</strong>, <strong>Left eye</strong>, <strong>Both eyes</strong> or <strong>Not specified</strong>. When it settles, <strong>End this episode</strong> offers one free line — <strong>How did it end?</strong> — in your own words. Both are optional, and both travel into your report, which now lists up to twelve episodes: <em>12 Mar → 20 Mar · Right eye</em>.'),problem:tr('“How many flares this year, and in which eye?” is the question that decides the treatment, and dates alone cannot answer it. Nothing is assumed either: an episode with no side recorded is printed with no side — never “both eyes” by inference.'),steps:[tr('Open the <strong>Eyes</strong> tracker and tap <strong>An episode is starting</strong>.'),tr('Pick the eye — or leave it <strong>Not specified</strong> — and tap <strong>Start the episode</strong>.'),tr('When it settles, tap <strong>End this episode</strong> and, if you like, write one line about how it ended.')],tip:tr('Light sensitivity now has its own line on your Flare Radar, beside eye comfort — it is the uveitis signal, where dryness is the dry-eye one.')},{title:tr('Your Glucose Diary'),shot:'img-glucose.png',where:[tr('Journal')],body:tr('If <strong>Diabetes</strong> is one of the conditions you follow, a <strong>Glucose Diary</strong> appears in your Journal, inside the <strong>Energy &amp; Rest</strong> group — and if it isn\u2019t, the same place offers a switch to turn the diary on anyway (and to hide it again). The first time you open it, you pick the unit your meter shows: <strong>mg/dL</strong> or <strong>mmol/L</strong>, each with an example number, and you can change it later from the small unit button beside the field. Every reading can carry a label — <strong>Fasting</strong>, <strong>Before meal</strong>, <strong>After meal</strong>, <strong>Bedtime</strong>, <strong>During the night</strong> or <strong>Low / hypo</strong> — and a second diary just below keeps the <strong>HbA1c</strong> from each lab report, in <strong>%</strong> or <strong>mmol/mol</strong>.'),problem:tr('Readings written on paper strips get lost, and units differ from country to country — 98 mg/dL and 5.4 mmol/L are the same blood. Every entry is kept with its own unit and its own label, so what reaches the PDF report your doctor reads is exactly what your meter said, with the context that makes it readable.'),steps:[tr('Open the <strong>Journal</strong> tab and scroll to <strong>Glucose Diary</strong>.'),tr('Pick your unit the first time — you can change it whenever you like.'),tr('Type the number, tap the label that fits, then tap <strong>Add entry</strong>. The <strong>HbA1c</strong> diary below works the same way.')],tip:tr('Chronic Friends never interprets these numbers: no ranges, no zones, no colours, no alerts, no advice. It is your own record of what you measured — and it travels into your PDF report.')},{title:tr('Skin photos stay on this phone'),where:[tr('Journal'),tr('Skin & Mouth')],body:tr('With the <strong>Skin &amp; Mouth</strong> tracker on, its <strong>Photo diary</strong> keeps photographs of the same spot over time: add a photo, say where on the body it is and on which day, and <strong>Compare</strong> puts two of them side by side. These photos never leave your phone \u2014 they are not uploaded, not synced, and never added to a report unless you choose to.'),problem:tr('A rash or a mouth ulcer looks different by the time the appointment comes round, and photographs of your own skin are as private as data gets. Keeping them on the device alone lets you show a change over weeks without handing your skin to a server.'),tip:tr('Nothing is uploaded, so nothing is backed up either: if you delete the app or change phone, these photos go with it. Keep the ones that matter in your phone\u2019s own photo gallery as well.')},{title:tr('Blood pressure keeps both numbers'),where:[tr('Journal'),tr('Breath & Heart')],body:tr('With the <strong>Breath &amp; Heart</strong> tracker on, its readings log takes your <strong>blood pressure</strong> the way you read it off your own monitor: <strong>Systolic</strong> and <strong>Diastolic</strong>, with a label like <strong>At rest</strong>. The list shows them as the pair you wrote (<strong>120/80</strong>) and the chart now draws <strong>both</strong> lines on one shared scale \u2014 the solid line is systolic, the dashed one below it is diastolic.'),problem:tr('Until now the chart drew only the first number, so a diary of 120/80 was shown as half the truth without ever saying so. Your record is your record: what you write down is what you get back.'),tip:tr('No ranges, no zones, no \u201cnormal\u201d or \u201chigh\u201d, no colour of alarm: the app keeps your numbers and never judges them. Reading them is your doctor\u2019s job.')}]},/* ---------- 4. DAILY REMINDER ---------- *//* 7 Sep 2026 — the reminder is ON by default at 21:03, it rings on flare
     days and on days already logged, and it opens the one-question-per-screen
     flow. Two ways to switch it off, and «Journal reminder» is the live
     dictionary's own string (i18nckflow).
     🔴 The shot: was REMOVED. assets/manual/img-ckrem.png shows the sheet at
     21:00 with «Remind me daily» — i.e. the old opt-in default, off. Showing
     it would teach the opposite of what the app now does (same rule that
     retired IMG_6952). The file stays in the folder as history; the topic
     waits for a fresh capture of the sheet, on, at 21:03. */{id:'ckrem',icon:'bellRing',grad:['#7bd853','#2e7d36'],title:tr('Your daily reminder'),intro:tr('A gentle evening nudge asking how your day went — for everyone, whether or not you take medication. It is on from the start, at 21:03.'),topics:[{title:tr('One soft chime, fully private'),where:[tr('Home'),tr('Check-In')],body:tr('One soft chime at the time you choose — never a loud alarm. The notification says only that your daily check-in is waiting: no symptom, no question and no condition name ever reaches your lock screen. Tapping it opens the evening questions, one per screen. It rings on flare days and on days you have already logged too — both are worth telling — and anything already answered appears already chosen, so nothing of yours is ever overwritten unseen.'),problem:tr('A journal only reveals patterns if it is filled in — and remembering to log is the hardest part of all. A gentle, private nudge builds the habit without ever exposing your health on a locked phone.'),steps:[tr('It is already on when you start, at <strong>21:03</strong> — three minutes after the hour most people set their medication for, so the two never collide.'),tr('Change the hour, or switch it off, on the first line at the top of your journal — or in the app settings, under <strong>Journal reminder</strong>. Both are the same switch.'),tr('Switching the journal reminder off never silences your medication alarms: those are a different switch.')],tip:tr('Off is off: switch it off and it stays off, updates included. And if your phone has notifications blocked, the app says so out loud instead of showing you a switch that cannot reach you.')}]},/* ---------- 5. FLARE RADAR ---------- */{id:'radar',icon:'pulse',grad:['#46e07e','#0f3d22'],title:tr('Flare Radar'),intro:tr('Reading the days you log — the radar turns your own diary into signals you can actually see and act on.'),topics:[{title:tr('Your radar, live'),shot:'IMG_6945.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Flare Radar')],body:tr('Everything you log becomes a dot on a live radar — <strong>green dots are helping</strong>, <strong>amber dots deserve a watch</strong>. Below the dial, the strongest signal of the day is named, with its <strong>flare push</strong> percentage and an honest one-line reading. It updates every time you log.'),problem:tr('Flares rarely arrive out of nowhere — they build quietly for days while everything still feels \u201cfine.\u201d The radar reads those early signals in your own data and gives you a head start you\u2019d otherwise never get.'),tip:tr('The radar is guidance, not a diagnosis. It can never replace your doctor.')},{title:tr('Act early & share with your doctor'),shot:'IMG_6946.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Flare Radar')],body:tr('Tap <strong>Full breakdown</strong> to see every driver — energy, screen time, steps, mindset, bowel visits and more — each with a <strong>Helping</strong> or <strong>Watch</strong> chip, a plain-words sentence and its share of the picture. The <strong>\u201cAppear on my doctor\u2019s radar\u201d</strong> switch will let verified doctors caring for you see your reading so they can reach out early, once video consultations are live.'),problem:tr('Knowing a flare is coming only helps if you can act — and if your doctor finds out before it\u2019s an emergency. This turns a warning into concrete next steps, and closes the silent gap between appointments.'),steps:[tr('Open <strong>Full breakdown</strong> and read the drivers to understand <strong>why</strong> today reads the way it does.'),tr('<strong>\u201cAppear on my doctor\u2019s radar\u201d</strong> lives in <strong>Settings \u203a Privacy & Data</strong>. It will share only your score — <strong>never your journal</strong> — and you will be able to turn it off anytime. It is not available yet: video consultations are still on the way, so tapping it explains that instead of switching anything on.')]}]},/* ---------- 6. FOOD SCAN ---------- */{id:'foodscan',icon:'cam',grad:['#f0b765','#d98a26'],title:tr('Food Scan'),intro:tr('A food record with almost no effort — scan a barcode today; recognising your plate from a photo is on its way.'),topics:[{title:tr('Scan barcode'),shot:'IMG_6950.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Food Scan')],body:tr('Tap <strong>Food Scan</strong> on Home and point the camera at a product\u2019s <strong>barcode</strong> — you get its real ingredients and nutrition, and the item lands on <strong>today\u2019s journal</strong>. You can also <strong>add meals manually</strong> or browse your <strong>food calendar</strong>. <strong>\u201cSnap your plate\u201d</strong> — recognising foods from one photo — is marked <strong>Coming soon</strong>: it isn\u2019t available yet.'),problem:tr('Working out which foods upset your gut is slow, tedious and easy to give up on. Scanning builds an honest food record with almost no effort — the record you need to match meals against symptoms and spot your real triggers.'),steps:[tr('On Home, tap <strong>Food Scan</strong>, then <strong>Scan barcode</strong>.'),tr('Point at the product\u2019s barcode and review its real ingredients and nutrition.'),tr('Or tap <strong>Add manually</strong> — and find everything again in the <strong>Food calendar</strong>.')],tip:tr('Your photo is analysed and then forgotten — it is never stored. Five barcode scans a month are free; Premium scans without a limit.')}]},/* ---------- 7. MEDICATION ---------- */{id:'meds',icon:'pill',grad:['#4fd0c4','#1fa596'],title:tr('Medication'),intro:tr('Reminders that actually ring, refill alerts before you run out, and an adherence record your doctor will love.'),topics:[{title:tr('Your meds & reminders'),shot:'IMG_6949.png',where:[tr('Medication')],body:tr('Keep every treatment in one place. The screen opens on a <strong>NEXT DOSE</strong> card with a live countdown, a one-tap <strong>Take now</strong> button and a ring showing how much of <strong>today</strong> is already taken. Below it, each medication shows its dose and schedule — and reminders <strong>ring like an alarm</strong> at the right time.'),problem:tr('Long-term treatment is easy to slip on — a missed injection or a forgotten evening dose can undo weeks of progress. Reminders that genuinely ring keep your treatment on track without you having to remember.'),steps:[tr('Open the <strong>Medication</strong> tab in the bottom bar.'),tr('Tap <strong>Add medication</strong> and enter name, dose and schedule.'),tr('When a dose rings, tap <strong>Take</strong> or <strong>Snooze</strong>.'),/* DEVOLUCIÓN-2 30 Aug 2026 §1 — the time chips of a medicine are a
             three-state cycle now (meds.jsx: unlogged → taken → skipped →
             unlogged), so ANY dose of today, including one whose hour has
             already gone, is one tap from any answer. The state words come
             from the live dictionary (Taken · Skipped, i18nmanual2026x). */tr('On each medicine, tap one of its times to log <strong>today</strong>’s dose: one tap marks it <strong>Taken</strong>, a second <strong>Skipped</strong>, a third leaves it unlogged again.'),tr('Several medicines at the same time ring as <strong>one</strong> alarm that lists them — tap <strong>I took them all</strong>, or <strong>Answer one by one</strong> to reply to each dose separately.')],tip:tr('A dose that changes every time, a treatment that steps down, or reminders that never arrive? The other guides in this chapter are about exactly that.')},/* 🔴 MED22 (8 Sep 2026) — a non-daily medication no longer offers a dose
         on the six days it is not due. This topic is the patient-facing half
         of that fix: what they will see instead, and why it matters that a
         phantom dose can never be marked again (it deducted a pen and reset
         the period, moving the real injection a week). The only two labels
         inside <strong> are «Medication» and «Not due today», both taken
         FROM the live dictionary (CF_UI_MAP: i18n · i18nmed22ux32) — the four
         cadences are written out in words on purpose, so no label the manual
         cannot verify in 16 languages is ever quoted.
         NO shot: — no capture of a non-due day exists yet. */{title:tr('A weekly injection, only on its day'),where:[tr('Medication'),tr('My medications')],body:tr('Not every treatment is daily. A biologic can be one injection a week, one every fifteen days, one a month or one every eight weeks — and Chronic Friends offers that dose only on the day it is really due. On the other days the medicine keeps its place in your list, with its frequency and its stock, and its card simply reads <strong>Not due today</strong>. The reminder and the screen always say the same thing, because both read the same calendar.'),problem:tr('A dose offered on the wrong day is not a small thing: marking it takes a pen out of your stock and starts the period over, so the real injection slides seven days while you believe you are up to date. Now a dose is shown only when it exists — and on the day it is due it rings, it appears on the card and it counts towards the day like any other.'),steps:[tr('In <strong>Medication</strong>, open the medicine and choose how often you take it: once a week, once every 15 days, once a month or every 8 weeks.'),tr('On the day it is due, log it exactly as you would a daily dose.'),tr('On the other days there is nothing to do: no dose is offered and the card says <strong>Not due today</strong>.')],tip:tr('The count starts again from the dose you log, so an injection taken a day late simply moves the next one a day too.')},{title:tr('Refills & adherence'),where:[tr('Medication')],body:tr('The app counts your remaining stock and warns you <strong>before you run out</strong> — you\u2019ll see a \u201cRunning low\u201d alert with days left, so there\u2019s time to order a refill. It also keeps your medication record: the share of doses you took on time, and your <strong>days in a row without forgetting</strong>.'),problem:tr('Running out of a long-term medication mid-treatment is genuinely risky, and \u201cAre you taking it as prescribed?\u201d is hard to answer honestly. Stock alerts prevent the panic, and your adherence number gives your doctor an accurate answer — it even feeds your Flare Radar.')},/* DEVOLUCIÓN 30 Aug 2026 §3 — a day already gone. The «Dose log» card is
         gone and its three abilities live in the calendar's day sheet now, all
         writing on the day being looked at (medcharts.jsx MedDaySheet). No
         shot: — no capture of the day sheet exists yet. */{title:tr('A dose from a day gone by'),where:[tr('Medication'),tr('Medication calendar')],body:tr('Tap any day in the <strong>Medication calendar</strong> and it opens with that day’s doses: mark each one <strong>Taken</strong> or <strong>Skipped</strong> and it is written on <strong>that</strong> day, never on today. The same day also carries the medicines you take <strong>As needed</strong> — <strong>Log dose</strong>, and on a day already gone you write the hour yourself — the amount field for a treatment whose dose changes each time, and <strong>Log the day</strong> to go through the whole day at once.'),problem:tr('If you took a painkiller yesterday and did not write it down, or you forgot the units of yesterday’s fast insulin, there was nowhere left to put them — and those units are exactly what your doctor reads in the report. Nothing is invented here: on a past day you type the hour of the dose yourself, and a dose you leave alone simply stays unlogged.'),steps:[tr('In <strong>Medication</strong>, scroll down to the <strong>Medication calendar</strong> and tap the day.'),tr('Mark each dose <strong>Taken</strong> or <strong>Skipped</strong> — tap the same button again to remove the mark.'),tr('For a medicine you take <strong>As needed</strong>, tap <strong>Log dose</strong> and write the hour.')],tip:tr('Today needs none of this: on the <strong>Next dose</strong> card, <strong>Take now</strong> and <strong>Skipped</strong> are one tap away.')},{title:tr('When the dose changes each time'),where:[tr('Medication'),tr('My medications')],body:tr('Some treatments have no fixed dose: fast insulin changes units at every meal, depending on what you are about to eat and how your glucose is. When you add or edit the medication, turn on <strong>The dose changes each time</strong> — the dose then reads <strong>as prescribed</strong> instead of a number. From then on, every dose you mark as <strong>Taken</strong> on the medication’s own card offers <strong>Add how much you took</strong>: write the amount and it stays on that dose (\u201c12 units\u201d). Tap it again to change it or remove it. For a medicine you only take when you need it, the same question waits for you in the <strong>Next dose</strong> card.'),problem:tr('\u201c1 injection\u201d tells your endocrinologist nothing \u2014 what they need is how many units you really injected, and at what time. The number travels straight into your PDF report, beside the hour of that dose. Every insulin in the catalogue arrives with the switch already on, and you can turn it off if your dose is fixed.'),steps:[tr('In <strong>Medication</strong>, add or edit the treatment and turn on <strong>The dose changes each time</strong>.'),tr('Mark the dose as <strong>Taken</strong> as you always do \u2014 the dose first, the number afterwards.'),tr('Tap <strong>Add how much you took</strong>, type the amount and tap <strong>Save</strong>.')],tip:tr('The number is always optional, and Chronic Friends never fills it in for you \u2014 it never suggests, calculates or corrects a dose. It only writes down what you say you took.')},{title:tr('A treatment that steps down and ends'),where:[tr('Medication')],body:tr('Many treatments are prescribed as a plan: a dose that goes down every few days and then stops. When you activate a medication, Chronic Friends asks <strong>Does this treatment step down and end?</strong> \u2014 answer <strong>Yes, set up the plan</strong> and write it exactly as your doctor gave it to you: <strong>8 per day for 7 days</strong>, <strong>6 per day for 7 days</strong>, and so on, one line per step. As you type you see the total length and the end date (\u201c35 days \u00b7 ends 19 September\u201d), which is what you check against what you were told. The doses are spread across your waking hours \u2014 a window you can move. From then on the reminders follow the plan: when a new step begins, the times change on their own, and inside the medication a <strong>Your treatment</strong> grid shows every day of the plan, how many doses were due and how many you logged.'),problem:tr('Until now a medication had one fixed frequency and no end date, so the phone would still be ringing eight times a day in week two. The app never proposes a plan, a dose or a step \u2014 that would be medical advice; you type in what your own doctor prescribed. When the last day passes, the reminders stop by themselves and one calm message tells you the treatment is complete: the medication stays in your list, ready to be re-activated if it is prescribed again. The plan and what you really took travel into your PDF report, and if your stock will not reach the end \u2014 8 drops a day empty a bottle fast \u2014 you are warned early, with the date.'),steps:[tr('Activate the medication, or open it and turn on <strong>Treatment with a step-down</strong>.'),tr('Fill one line per step: doses <strong>per day</strong> and how many <strong>days</strong> \u2014 tap <strong>Add step</strong> for the next one.'),tr('Check the length and the end date, set the first day, and tap <strong>Start the treatment</strong>.'),tr('Set the hours to spread the doses between: the first dose of the day rings at the start of your window and the last one exactly at its end \u2014 never outside it.')],tip:tr('Nothing you already logged can be changed by a plan: editing or ending a treatment never touches a dose you have recorded.')},{title:tr('When there are many doses a day'),where:[tr('Medication'),tr('Next dose')],body:tr('A step-down can mean twelve reminders a day, so they are grouped: medicines that share an hour arrive as <strong>one</strong> notification saying how many doses are due, and an older one you never answered disappears from the tray when the next arrives \u2014 without marking anything as missed. Your lock screen only ever shows the count (\u201c3 doses now\u201d); the medication name appears once you open the app, and <strong>Show medication names on the lock screen</strong> in <strong>Alarms &amp; notifications</strong> is there if you prefer it the other way. Under <strong>Reminders</strong> you choose how each medication speaks to you: <strong>Every dose</strong>, a <strong>Daily summary</strong> (one in the morning with the day\u2019s plan, one in the evening to confirm) or <strong>Mixed</strong>.'),problem:tr('Eighty notifications a week is not care, it is noise \u2014 and a medication name on a locked screen is readable by anyone standing next to you. Doses you leave unticked simply stay unlogged: silence is never counted as a missed dose, and nothing you skip is ever shown in alarm red.'),steps:[tr('Tap a grouped notification and the app opens exactly those doses, ready to answer — silently, because you have already seen it.'),tr('In <strong>Medication</strong>, tap <strong>Reminders</strong> on a medication and pick <strong>Every dose</strong>, <strong>Daily summary</strong> or <strong>Mixed</strong>.'),tr('Set the hour of the <strong>Morning summary</strong> and the <strong>Evening check</strong> if you chose one of those.'),tr('At night, open <strong>Log the day</strong> and tick what you took.')],tip:tr('The summary is deliberately soft \u2014 one gentle chime, never the full alarm screen. That one is kept for the doses themselves.')},{title:tr('Which eye — and the bottle that empties twice as fast'),where:[tr('Medication')],body:tr('Eye drops arrive with <strong>no dose written in</strong>: how many drops, and in which eye, is your prescription — not something the app may assume. When you activate a drop, or edit it later, you pick <strong>Right eye</strong>, <strong>Left eye</strong>, <strong>Both eyes</strong> or <strong>Not specified</strong>, and nothing is selected for you. Whatever you choose then travels with the dose everywhere it is shown: the medication card, the alarm, the dose log and the PDF your ophthalmologist reads. Choose <strong>Both eyes</strong> and the app also counts <strong>two drops per dose</strong> against your bottle, because that is what really leaves it.'),problem:tr('Anterior uveitis is usually one eye, so a pre-written \u201c1 drop each eye\u201d was the app stating a clinical fact you never gave it \u2014 the same reason your weight and age stay empty until you fill them in. And counting one drop when you use two is not a rounding error: a 5 ml bottle holds roughly 100 drops, so at eight doses a day it empties around day 12, not day 24 \u2014 halfway through a five-week taper, with a weekend in between.'),steps:[tr('Activate an eye drop, or open it and tap <strong>Which eye?</strong>'),tr('Pick the side your prescription says \u2014 or leave it <strong>Not specified</strong>.'),tr('Type how many drops in <strong>Dose</strong> if your prescription says a number.')],tip:tr('Left unspecified, the side simply does not appear anywhere \u2014 not on the card, not in the report. Absence is printed as absence.')},{title:tr('Check your reminders will arrive'),shot:'img-notifhealth.png',where:[tr('Medication')],body:tr('A reminder that never rings is worse than no reminder at all \u2014 and every Android phone hides the switches somewhere different. In <strong>Medication</strong>, open <strong>Will your reminders reach you?</strong>: the screen names your own next dose \u2014 the medicine and the hour \u2014 and answers in three words, <strong>It will sound</strong> or <strong>It will NOT sound</strong>. When something is blocking it there is a single button, <strong>Fix it now</strong>, which opens the exact screen on your phone for that one problem; solve it and the next one, if there is one, takes its place. <strong>Ring it now, so you hear it</strong> makes your phone ring on the spot, so you never have to take our word for it. Everything the app looked at \u2014 notifications, exact timing, battery saving, the reminder sound, the note for Xiaomi, Huawei, OPPO and vivo phones, and how many reminders this phone is holding \u2014 is folded under <strong>See what was checked</strong>. If your phone cannot deliver them, a small card on the Medication screen says so.'),problem:tr('Silence is the one failure this app cannot afford, and we cannot guess how your phone is configured \u2014 so the app asks it and tells you honestly. On Xiaomi, Huawei, OPPO and vivo phones a short note points at the setting that stops apps in the background. Nothing we do not know is ever shown as a problem.'),steps:[tr('In <strong>Medication</strong>, tap <strong>Will your reminders reach you?</strong>'),tr('Read the answer for your next dose \u2014 if it says <strong>It will NOT sound</strong>, tap <strong>Fix it now</strong>.'),tr('Tap <strong>Ring it now, so you hear it</strong> and listen.')],tip:tr('This screen never changes your reminders \u2014 it only looks and opens your phone\u2019s settings. On a computer there is nothing to check: reminders ring in the phone app.')}]},/* ---------- 8. MEDITATION ---------- */{id:'meditation',icon:'moon',grad:['#b09cf0','#5b48a8'],title:tr('Meditation'),intro:tr('Small rituals for body, mind & spirit — a calm room whenever you need it.'),topics:[{title:tr('A calm room'),shot:'IMG_6953.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Meditation')],body:tr('Ten little doors, and behind each one a few quiet minutes: breathing that moves with you, grounding, gentle rituals for body, mind and spirit. Star your favourites to keep them at the top. Nothing here measures you — everything here holds you.'),problem:tr('Stress and symptoms feed each other, yet most meditation apps demand streaks and stats — one more thing to fail at. This room asks nothing of you: open a door, take your minutes, leave whenever you like.'),steps:[tr('Open <strong>Meditation</strong> from its card on Home.'),tr('Pick a door — each one explains itself in a single line.'),tr('Tap the <strong>star</strong> to keep a ritual in your favourites.')],tip:tr('One session a day is free; Premium opens the room as often as you like.')}]},/* ---------- 9. ENTERTAINMENT ---------- */{id:'games',icon:'tv',grad:['#62b5f0','#2f7fd4'],title:tr('Entertainment'),intro:tr('Ten little offline games to rest and unwind with — gentle, private, never scored against you.'),topics:[{title:tr('Games for the hard hours'),shot:'img-entertainment.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Entertainment')],body:tr('A small arcade that works offline: quiet games made for tired days — no ads, no timers, no leaderboards. Open it from the <strong>Entertainment</strong> card on Home; during Flare Mode a calmer selection sits right on the shelter dashboard.'),problem:tr('Waiting rooms, infusion chairs and flare days are full of empty, anxious time. A pocket of gentle distraction passes that time without demanding energy you don\u2019t have.'),steps:[tr('Tap <strong>Entertainment</strong> on Home.'),tr('Pick a game — everything works offline.'),tr('In Flare Mode, look for the games card on the shelter dashboard.')],tip:tr('One game a day is free; Premium plays without a counter.')}]},/* ---------- 10. TOOLS ---------- */{id:'tools',icon:'book',grad:['#8f9df0','#4a5cc4'],title:tr('Tools'),intro:tr('A small library for everyday life — ways of looking at things, take what serves you.'),topics:[{title:tr('Ways of seeing'),shot:'IMG_6955.png',where:[tr('Home'),tr('Tools')],body:tr('Short, self-guided exercises — like <strong>The Seven Mirrors</strong>, seven honest questions to know yourself better, or lenses on <strong>interpretation</strong>, <strong>perspective</strong> and <strong>perception</strong>. Each card explains itself in a line; star the ones you return to. A growing collection, shaped by what you ask for.'),problem:tr('Living with a condition colours how every setback reads. These are small mindset practices that sit <strong>beside</strong> your healthcare, never in place of it — a wider way to look when the day narrows.'),steps:[tr('Open <strong>Tools</strong> from its card on Home.'),tr('Browse the sections and open any card that speaks to you.'),tr('Tap the <strong>star</strong> to keep it under Favorites.')],tip:tr('Tools is free for everybody, with no limit at all.')},{title:tr('The Tools room'),where:[tr('Home'),tr('Tools'),tr('Tools room')],body:tr('At the top of <strong>Tools</strong>, a blue door opens the <strong>Tools room</strong> — a shared space to talk about these tools. It works like the Community wall: write a post, read the others, comment. What changes is the tag. Nobody comes here to talk about a diagnosis, so instead of conditions you pick one of fourteen topics — from <strong>Awareness</strong> and <strong>Ego</strong> to <strong>Letting go</strong>, <strong>Boundaries</strong> and <strong>Calmness</strong>. It is optional and nothing is ever pre-selected; tap the same tag twice and it comes off. Once tagged posts exist, a row of chips appears above the wall so you can read one topic at a time — <strong>All</strong> brings everything back.'),problem:tr('A list of illnesses was the wrong question in this room. Topic tags let a thought find the people already thinking about the same thing, without anyone having to say a word about their body.'),steps:[tr('Open <strong>Tools</strong> on Home and tap <strong>Tools room</strong>.'),tr('Tap <strong>New Post</strong>, pick a tool tag if one fits, and write.'),tr('Use the chips above the wall to read one topic — or <strong>All</strong>.')],tip:tr('The tags name a topic, never a task: somewhere to leave a thought, not something to work on.')}]},/* ---------- 11. HEALTH SYNC ---------- */{id:'healthsync',icon:'heart',grad:['#5fc0af','#2f8a7d'],title:tr('Health Sync'),intro:tr('Your phone already counts your steps and your sleep. Let the app read them and that part of your diary fills in by itself — free for everyone.'),topics:[{title:tr('Connect your health data'),shot:'img-healthsync.png',where:[tr('Settings'),tr('Connect your health data')],body:tr('The invitation asks it plainly: <strong>\u201cShall we count your steps and sleep for you?\u201d</strong> Tap <strong>Yes, read them from my phone</strong> and your phone\u2019s own permission screen decides what the app may read — you allow it scope by scope. Chronic Friends is <strong>read-only</strong>: it never writes anything back. From then on your steps and sleep arrive <strong>already filled in</strong> in the daily check-in — you can edit any of them by hand, and your own entry always wins — they show up in Analytics, and they feed your Flare Radar like any other entry. Settings keeps a <strong>\u201cToday, from your phone\u201d</strong> panel with what arrived today.'),problem:tr('Steps, sleep and activity shape how a day with a chronic condition goes, but logging them by hand is busywork your phone already did. Letting the app read them fills that part of the journal for you — honestly, and only with your permission.'),steps:[tr('On Home — or in Settings — open <strong>Connect your health data</strong> and tap <strong>Yes, read them from my phone</strong>.'),tr('Allow what you\u2019re comfortable with on your phone\u2019s own screen.'),tr('That\u2019s it — synced days appear in your journal automatically.')],tip:tr('Read-only, always. Chronic Friends never writes anything back, and you choose what to allow on your phone\u2019s own screen.')},{title:tr('Your steps on Home'),where:[tr('Home'),tr('Steps today')],body:tr('Just above the <strong>Check-In</strong> button, Home always shows <strong>Steps today</strong>, and that number is a button: tap it and the <strong>Step tournament</strong> opens, with its four periods — today, this week, this month and this year. It opens with or without a health app connected: you read the whole table, you are simply not in it, and a line inside offers to connect your steps. With no number the card shows a dash, never a zero, and it says which of the two silences it is: nothing connected, or nothing recorded today.'),problem:tr('The tournament was already inside the app and nobody could reach it: the only door was four taps deep in Settings, and it vanished completely for anyone who had not connected a health app. A number sitting on Home, that opens when you tap it, is a door.'),tip:tr('A dash means we do not know — never that you walked nothing. The app never shows a number your phone did not give it.')}]},/* ---------- 12. COMMUNITY ----------
     BUILD 1.0.3 §8: the step tournament MOVED here from Health Sync (nobody
     looks for a game inside «health synchronisation») with its text exactly
     as it was translated — the two doors it names, Home and Settings ›
     Health data, are the real ones. «Your steps on Home» stays in the Health
     Sync chapter, where the card itself is explained. */{id:'community',icon:'users',grad:['#62b5f0','#2f7fd4'],title:tr('Community'),intro:tr('People who truly get it. Posts, friends and private chats — this is the heart that gives the app its name.'),topics:[{title:tr('Posts & the feed'),shot:'IMG_6951.png',where:[tr('Community'),tr('Posts')],body:tr('Open <strong>Community</strong> and you land on <strong>Posts</strong> — a feed where patients (and verified doctors) share experiences, questions and small wins. Tap <strong>\u2764</strong> to support a post, the bubble to comment, or the big <strong>New Post</strong> button to share your own. The tabs across the top switch between Posts, Chronic Friends, Best Chronic Friends and Doctors.'),problem:tr('Chronic illness can be isolating, and friends who haven\u2019t lived it often just don\u2019t understand. Here you\u2019re surrounded by people who instantly do — advice, reassurance and \u201cme too\u201d from people walking the same road.')},/* BUILD 37 (1 Sep 2026) — the row of a person in the census lost its
         compact «Add as friend» button: it ate the width and cut the names
         («Carl…», «Euge…»). The ask lives on the card now, so the body and
         the second step say so. The two old strings stay orphans in
         i18nmanual2026i.jsx — index-addressed dictionaries are never pruned.
         Every <strong> button name below is the live dictionary's own. */{title:tr('Friends, best friends & chats'),where:[tr('Community'),tr('Chats')],body:tr('You reach a person in two ways. In the <strong>Chronic Friends</strong> tab, type the start of a name in the search field: people who are not your friends yet appear below your own list, under <strong>Other people</strong> \u2014 tap one to open their card and ask from there. Or tap the name of whoever wrote a post and ask from their profile. When they accept, they appear in your <strong>Chronic Friends</strong> tab and you can write to each other in a private 1-to-1 chat. Requests you receive wait at the top of <strong>Community</strong>, with a red dot on the tab: <strong>Accept</strong> or <strong>Decline</strong>. Declining is quiet \u2014 nothing is announced to anyone. Tap the <strong>\u2b50</strong> on a friend to add them to your <strong>Best Chronic Friends</strong>, your inner circle in its own tab.'),problem:tr('Big feeds are noisy, and a flare at 3am needs one person, not a crowd. Best friends and private chats give you a small, trusted circle you can reach directly, whenever you need them.'),steps:[tr('In <strong>Chronic Friends</strong>, type the start of a name \u2014 or, in <strong>Posts</strong>, tap the name of whoever wrote it.'),tr('Tap the person to open their card, then <strong>Add as friend</strong> \u2014 the button then reads <strong>Friend request sent</strong> until they answer.'),tr('Once you are friends, open their profile and tap <strong>Write a message</strong> \u2014 or find them in the <strong>Chronic Friends</strong> tab.')],tip:tr('Private chats are between accepted friends only, patient to patient \u2014 nobody can write to you out of the blue. Doctors share guidance through posts and comments instead.')},/* BUILD 1.0.3 §1/§8 — the tab no longer waits for anybody to type. The
         topic above keeps its own words on purpose: search results still live
         under «Other people», so nothing in it became false. */{title:tr('Everybody is in the list'),where:[tr('Community'),tr('All people')],body:tr('Open the <strong>Chronic Friends</strong> tab and, below your own friends, <strong>All people</strong> lists everyone who is on Chronic Friends. Keep scrolling and more names arrive, twenty at a time. Tap a name to read that person\u2019s profile and ask for their friendship from there, or use the search field above to narrow the list \u2014 it matches the <strong>start</strong> of a name, so \u201cmar\u201d finds \u201cMarta\u201d and not \u201cAna Mar\u00eda\u201d. Being in the list only means people can ask: nobody can write to you until you have accepted them.'),problem:tr('A new account used to open this tab, see an empty friends list and conclude there was nobody here. The people are the app \u2014 so they are visible from the first second, without typing a letter.'),tip:tr('If you would rather not appear there, turn off <strong>Let others find me</strong> in Settings \u2014 your current friends and chats stay exactly as they are.')},/* ANNEX 1 (29 Aug 2026) — the switch takes you out of the LIST, not
         only out of search: the census publishes everybody without anyone
         typing. Same correction as Privacy v3.0 §4; the old body stays an
         orphan key in i18nmanual2026j.jsx. */{title:tr('Being found by other people'),where:[tr('Settings')],body:tr('Every member sees the list of people without typing anything, and the search field only narrows it — it matches the <strong>start</strong> of a name, so “mar” finds “Marta” and not “Ana María”. Being in the list only lets people ask: <strong>nobody can write to you until you have accepted them as a friend</strong>. If you would rather not be there at all, turn off <strong>Settings › Let others find me</strong> — you disappear from the list and from search, while your current friends and chats stay exactly as they are.'),problem:tr('Being searchable helps the people who already know you find you \u2014 and is unwelcome on the days you would rather stay quiet. One switch decides it, and it never touches the friendships you already have.')},/* IDS (1 Sep 2026) — the profile card used to manufacture a country, an
         age, the years with the illness and a bio out of a hash of the
         account id whenever the person had not filled them in, and painted a
         green «Online now» dot for everybody. Both are gone. This topic says
         what the card really shows, so nobody expects the missing lines
         back. IDS-2 (3 Sep 2026): the body and the tip now separate FILLING
         A FIELD IN from PUBLISHING it — the four fields reach a card only
         through the «Show my details on my profile» switch, which is born
         off, so «only what they filled in» was true of nobody's card.
         NO shot: — no capture of the new card exists yet. */{title:tr('What a person’s profile shows'),where:[tr('Community'),tr('All people')],body:tr('Tap a name and that person’s card opens: their photo, their name, and then only what they filled in <em>and chose to publish</em> — their country, their age, the years they have lived with their condition, a line about themselves. All of that is optional twice over: they have to write it, and they have to turn on <strong>Show my details on my profile</strong>. So a card with only a photo and a name is complete, not broken. The green dot with <strong>Online now</strong> appears only while that person really is connected; when the app cannot tell, it shows nothing rather than guess.'),problem:tr('A card that fills its gaps with invented details is not a person — it is a fiction wearing someone’s face. Whatever Chronic Friends does not know about another member is not drawn: never an age, a country or a bio worked out from anything.'),tip:tr('Your own card follows the same rule: whatever you leave empty when you edit your profile in Settings simply does not appear. And filling something in is not the same as publishing it — your country, your age, your years with the condition and your bio only reach your card once you turn on <strong>Show my details on my profile</strong> in Settings › Privacy & Data.')},/* ANNEX 2 (29 Aug 2026) — the report door for PEOPLE. The engine
         (reports.jsx) always knew targetType "user"; until now nothing
         opened it, so a census that publishes everyone's name and photo
         had no way to report anybody. Block is documented with it because
         they are the same two buttons on the same card. */{title:tr('Blocking or reporting a person'),where:[tr('Community'),tr('All people')],body:tr('Open a person’s profile — from the list, from a post or from a comment — and under the friendship button there are two quiet buttons. <strong>Block user</strong> hides that person from you everywhere: their posts, their comments and their messages. It is always reversible, under <strong>Blocked users</strong> in <strong>Settings</strong>. <strong>Report user</strong> sends our team a reason — dangerous medical advice, harassment, spam or something else — and anything you want to add. The person is never told, and reporting does not hide them: if you also want them out of your app, block them as well.'),problem:tr('Safety cannot wait for a friendship. Anyone whose name and photo you can see in the list, you can also block or report from the same card, in two taps, without explaining yourself to anybody.'),tip:tr('Reporting is a safety tool, not an accusation — our team reads every report and decides what to do with it.')},{title:tr('The step tournament'),where:[tr('Settings'),tr('Health data'),tr('Steps')],body:tr('In <strong>Settings \u203a Health data</strong>, the <strong>Steps</strong> number is now a button: tap it and the tournament opens. Two lists \u2014 <strong>Friends</strong> and <strong>Global</strong> \u2014 across four periods: <strong>Today</strong>, <strong>Week</strong>, <strong>Month</strong> and <strong>Year</strong>, and every 1 January the year starts from zero again for everybody. Only people whose health app is connected can appear, because the number has to be a real one. <strong>Nobody takes part until they say yes</strong>: the first time you open it, a card explains that taking part publishes exactly three things \u2014 your name, your avatar and your step totals \u2014 and nothing else. The switch inside the window takes you out again whenever you want, and your entry is deleted from the table. It is free for everyone, and you can watch the table without taking part.'),problem:tr('Walking is easier with company, and a small game with people who understand is good company. What must never happen is health data landing on a list you never agreed to \u2014 so the tournament asks first, publishes only those three things, and lets you leave with one tap.'),steps:[tr('Open <strong>Settings \u203a Health data</strong> and tap the <strong>Steps</strong> number.'),tr('Read what would be published, then choose <strong>Take part</strong> \u2014 or <strong>Just have a look</strong>.'),tr('Switch between <strong>Friends</strong> and <strong>Global</strong>, and between <strong>Today</strong>, <strong>Week</strong>, <strong>Month</strong> and <strong>Year</strong>.')],tip:tr('It is a game, not a target: nobody is ever called last, and on a flare day a small number is simply a small number.')}]},/* ---------- 13. DOCTORS ---------- */{id:'doctors',icon:'doc',grad:['#b09cf0','#7556d0'],title:tr('Doctors & appointments (coming soon)'),intro:tr('Not available yet. Booking a specialist and video visits are on the way — this chapter is a preview of how they will work.'),topics:[{title:tr('Find a verified doctor'),where:[tr('Community'),tr('Doctors')],body:tr('Open <strong>Community \u203a Doctors</strong> to browse specialists — gastroenterology, nutrition, mental health and more. Every doctor\u2019s identity and medical licence are verified before they appear, and each card shows real patient reviews and a <strong>\u201cLicensed in \u2026\u201d</strong> badge.'),problem:tr('Finding a specialist who really understands your condition — and getting an appointment — is slow and stressful. Here every doctor is pre-vetted, so you skip the guesswork and the waiting room.'),tip:tr('For your safety you only ever see doctors licensed in <strong>your own country</strong>. Change your country at the top of the directory or in Settings.')},{title:tr('Book & join a video visit'),where:[tr('Community'),tr('Doctors'),tr('Book')],body:tr('Tap <strong>Book</strong> on a doctor\u2019s card, pick a free slot, and send the request. Once the doctor confirms, the visit appears on your <strong>Home</strong>. At the scheduled time tap <strong>Join video call</strong> — inside the call you can chat and <strong>share your health report</strong> so the doctor sees your journal summary while you talk.'),problem:tr('Travelling to appointments while unwell is exhausting, and doctors often work blind without your history. A video visit meets you where you are — and hands the specialist your real data instead of a rushed verbal summary.'),steps:[tr('On a doctor\u2019s card, tap <strong>Book</strong> and choose a <strong>date and time</strong>.'),tr('Send the request — you\u2019ll see it as <strong>Awaiting reply</strong>.'),tr('When it\u2019s confirmed, tap <strong>Join video call</strong> at the scheduled time.')]},{title:tr('Reviews & ratings'),where:[tr('Community'),tr('Doctors'),tr('Reviews')],body:tr('A doctor\u2019s star rating is always the <strong>average of real patient reviews</strong> — never a number we invent. After a consultation you can leave your own stars and a few words.'),problem:tr('Choosing a doctor online usually means trusting a stranger\u2019s marketing. Honest reviews from patients who live with conditions like yours let you choose based on real experiences.')}]},/* ---------- 14. PREMIUM ---------- */{id:'premium',icon:'spark',grad:['#f0b24a','#cc8418'],title:tr('Premium'),intro:tr('Every screen opens for everyone. Premium does not unlock doors — it takes the limits off.'),topics:[{title:tr('What Premium changes'),where:[tr('Settings'),tr('Membership')],body:tr('Nothing in Chronic Friends is locked away, and writing things down is never limited. On the free plan you read back your <strong>last 14 days</strong>, export reports of <strong>3, 7 or 14 days</strong>, scan <strong>5 barcodes a month</strong>, and open <strong>one game and one meditation a day</strong> on Home \u2014 with <strong>one more of each inside Flare Mode</strong>, counted separately. Premium takes those counters off: your whole history and reports of up to two years. <strong>Tools</strong> has no limit at all, for everybody, and Food Scan keeps the one ceiling nobody escapes: <strong>60 scans a calendar month</strong>, on every plan.'),problem:tr('\u201cFree\u201d apps that quietly lock the basics are exhausting. Here every door is open and your own records are never held back — what you pay for is depth: your whole history instead of a fortnight, and the long reports a doctor asks for.'),steps:[tr('The four plans: <strong>167,99 US$ / year</strong> \u00b7 <strong>87,99 US$ / 6 months</strong> \u00b7 <strong>19,99 US$ / month</strong> \u00b7 <strong>599,99 US$</strong> lifetime, one-time.'),tr('Your <strong>30-day trial</strong> counts as Premium — everything is open. The lifetime plan has no trial.'),tr('Two days before a trial ends, your phone shows a reminder with the date and the plan that will be charged — you hear about a charge before it happens, never after.'),tr('<strong>Settings \u203a Membership</strong> \u2192 <strong>Manage membership</strong> opens your subscription in the App Store or Google Play, the only place a plan can be changed or cancelled. On a computer there is nothing to open \u2014 Premium is managed on your phone.')],tip:tr('Recording is never limited — not one day, not one entry, on any plan.')},/* CHAT1a (1 Sep 2026) — the membership card is a tappable row now and
         opens «Your membership». No shot: yet — no capture of the new sheet
         exists. Every <strong> label below is the live dictionary string. */{title:tr('Your membership, in one place'),where:[tr('Settings'),tr('Membership')],body:tr('Tap <strong>Membership</strong> in Settings — on any plan, a gifted one included — and «<strong>Your membership</strong>» opens: what you have right now, the free plan with every counter it really has, what Premium changes, the four plans at the price the store will charge, and the way to manage or restore a purchase.'),problem:tr('Until now that card showed one sentence and a button to pay: nobody could read that the app is free for ever, and anybody whose access was a gift saw a card with nothing to tap at all.'),steps:[tr('<strong>The free plan</strong> is a list, not small print: journal, trackers, medication and community with no limit \u00b7 your last 14 days \u00b7 5 food scans a month \u00b7 one game and one meditation a day on Home, and one more of each in Flare Mode \u00b7 downloading your own data, always free.'),tr('Coming back to the same room within <strong>30 minutes</strong> is the same session — it does not spend another use. Food Scan is the exception: a scan is a scan.'),tr('The table of plans starts with the <strong>Free plan</strong>: <strong>Forever</strong>, <strong>Free</strong>, and nothing to tap because there is nothing to buy. A discreet <strong>Your plan</strong> marks the one you are on.'),tr('The four plans carry the price <strong>the store</strong> will charge, in your currency; tapping one opens the purchase sheet, where Apple or Google take over. <strong>Restore purchase</strong> is on the same screen.')],tip:tr('If your access was a gift — a pioneer year — the sheet says so first, with the date, and tells you there is nothing to buy today.')}]},/* ---------- 15. SETTINGS & PRIVACY ---------- */{id:'settings',icon:'gear',grad:['#9aa8b5','#5f7080'],title:tr('Settings & privacy'),intro:tr('Tap your avatar on Home to open Settings — your profile, language, data and safety all live here.'),topics:[{title:tr('Everything in Settings'),shot:'img-settings.png',where:[tr('Settings')],body:tr('Settings gathers your <strong>profile and avatar</strong>, your <strong>health details</strong>, your <strong>membership</strong>, language, notifications, privacy controls and help — all behind your avatar. Choose how you appear: upload a photo, pick a colour with your initials, or keep the default portrait.'),problem:tr('An app that manages your health needs to bend to you, not the other way around. One clear place to control how the app looks, speaks and behaves keeps you in charge.')},{title:tr('Your data is yours'),where:[tr('Settings'),tr('Privacy & Data')],body:tr('Under <strong>Privacy & Data</strong> you decide what your data may be used for, <strong>export</strong> everything as one file, <strong>change your password</strong>, or <strong>delete your account</strong> — permanently, with everything erased within 30 days. Tap <strong>Export my data</strong> and your phone opens its own share sheet, so you choose where the file goes: Files, e-mail, or any app you trust.'),problem:tr('Health data is deeply personal, and most apps make it a one-way street. Here it stays yours: you can see it, take it with you, or remove it entirely, at any time.'),tip:tr('Other members only ever see your name and your photo — plus anything you switch on yourself. Your journal is never one of those things.')},/* IDS-2 (1 Sep 2026) — the second permission. «Let others find me»
         was consent for a name and a photo; an age and the years with an
         illness are health data and now have their own switch, born off.
         The two button names inside <strong> come from the live
         dictionary. NO shot: — no capture of the new row exists yet. */{title:tr('Showing your details on your profile'),where:[tr('Settings'),tr('Privacy & Data')],body:tr('Two different permissions sit side by side. <strong>Let others find me</strong> puts your name and your photo in the list of members. <strong>Show my details on my profile</strong> is the other one, and it stays off until you turn it on: only then can other people see your country, your age, the years you have lived with your condition and the line you wrote about yourself. Right under the switch you can read exactly what would be published, before you decide.'),problem:tr('An age and the years with an illness are health data, and being findable was never permission to publish them. Two different questions deserve two different answers — and the one about your health starts as a no.'),/* 🔇 UX32 (8 Sep 2026) — the switch used to slide back to off in
           silence when the publish could not happen. Now it says what and
           why, so the manual says it too. Every <strong> label here comes
           FROM the live dictionary (Settings · Privacy & Data · the switch
           name of i18nprofileshare). */steps:[tr('In <strong>Settings</strong> › <strong>Privacy &amp; Data</strong>, turn on <strong>Show my details on my profile</strong> — and read, just below it, exactly what would be published.'),tr('If the switch goes back off by itself, the line underneath says why — no connection, not signed in, or an e-mail that is not verified yet — and nothing was published.')],tip:tr('Turning it off does not hide those details, it deletes them. And if you have not filled any of them in, the switch simply publishes nothing.')},{title:tr('Language, help & emergencies'),where:[tr('Settings'),tr('Language')],body:tr('Chronic Friends speaks <strong>16 languages</strong> — pick yours in Settings \u203a Language and the whole app switches instantly, including right-to-left layouts. The <strong>Help center</strong> answers common questions and reaches our care team, and <strong>Emergency info</strong> lists urgent-care numbers: <strong>112</strong> (EU) \u00b7 <strong>911</strong> (US) \u00b7 <strong>999</strong> (UK).'),problem:tr('A health companion is useless in a language you don\u2019t think in — and dangerous if it pretends to handle emergencies. Chronic Friends meets you in your own language and is always clear about when to seek urgent care instead.'),tip:tr('Chronic Friends can never contact emergency services for you. Severe pain, persistent vomiting, high fever or heavy bleeding \u2192 seek urgent care immediately.')}]}];}window.cfManualPatient=cfManualPatient;
})();